# {{name}}: static site on {{domain}}

Rendered by infralift from the `static-site-cdn` stack into one AWS account.
The bucket is in `{{region}}`; the certificate is in us-east-1, the only
region CloudFront takes certificates from. State: {{state_tool}}.

## What it creates

- `modules/site-bucket`: a private S3 bucket for the site's files. Public
  access blocked, encrypted, versioned (old versions kept 30 days).
- `modules/cdn`: a CloudFront distribution ({{price_class}}) on
  `https://{{domain}}`. It reads the bucket through origin access control, so
  the bucket policy admits this distribution and nothing else. An ACM
  certificate validated by DNS in the Route 53 zone `{{zone}}`, TLS 1.2 or
  later, HTTP redirected to HTTPS, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, compression, AWS's
  managed security headers, and a CloudFront Function that serves
  `index.html` for directory paths. A missing file answers 404 with your
  `/404.html`. A and AAAA alias records point the domain at the
  distribution.

The zone `{{zone}}` must already exist as a public hosted zone in this
account, and `{{domain}}` must be the zone or a name inside it.

## Publishing the site

infralift builds where the site is served from, not the site. From your
build output:

```sh
aws s3 sync ./dist s3://<bucket> --delete
aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id <distribution_id> --paths '/*'
```

## Cost

An estimate at list prices, checked 2026-11-14, not a quote. CloudFront's
always-free tier covers 1 TB of transfer, 10 million requests and 2 million
function invocations a month; S3 storage is about $0.023 per GB-month; the
certificate is free, and alias queries to CloudFront are free. A small site
costs well under $1/month. The hosted zone ($0.50/month) is not created
here.

## Credentials

{{#if backend_r2}}| Variable | Where | What |
|---|---|---|
| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | repository secrets (read-only key), `production` environment secrets (write key) | The AWS account this project manages |
| `TFSTATE_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `TFSTATE_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | repository secrets | An R2 API token's S3 credentials, object read and write on the state bucket |
| `TF_STATE_PASSPHRASE` | repository secret | Encrypts state and saved plans. Keep a copy: without it the state is unreadable |
| `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` | repository variable | The Cloudflare account that holds the state bucket |
{{/if}}{{#if backend_tfc}}| Variable | Where | What |
|---|---|---|
| `TF_API_TOKEN` | repository secret | A Terraform Cloud token that can plan and apply in workspace `{{tfc_workspace}}` |
| `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | workspace environment variables (sensitive) | The AWS account this project manages |
{{/if}}
## First apply

{{#if backend_r2}}1. Create the state bucket once, from `bootstrap/`:
   `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=... tofu init && tofu apply -var cloudflare_account_id=...`
2. Set the credentials above, then merge this {{change_request}}. The push to
   main plans, and the `production` environment applies that saved plan.
{{/if}}{{#if backend_tfc}}1. Set the credentials above on the repository and the workspace, then merge
   this {{change_request}}. The push to main plans in Terraform Cloud, and the
   `production` environment confirms that run.
{{/if}}
A daily `infra drift` job plans main against what is running and fails if
anything changed outside Terraform.
